            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/89/81589/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Jason Hsu",
                "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
            },
            "createdOn": 1751442078,
            "author": {
                "name": "Jason Hsu",
                "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "configurations/meta/ventura_fanboard_adc_max.json",
                    "line": 293,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why do we need the same information represented twice?  Can't we use `$bus % 2` in the Name field so we only need one copy of this?  I don't think we should have to explicitly reference bus 16/17 in a probe statement."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/meta/ventura_fanboard_adc_max.json",
                    "line": 293,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "This modification is a special case. The EE team requires a new fan board, and this new fan board needs a new formula to calculate the sensor voltage value. However, the old fan board cannot apply this formula. Therefore, we have to probe it twice based on the different AM4 values.\nAdditionally, if we don't explicitly reference bus 16/17 in the probe statement, it's likely that bus 16/17 may not consistently correspond to fan 0/1 when probing the fan board. If my understanding is incorrect, please feel free to let me know, thank you."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/meta/ventura_fanboard_adc_max.json",
                    "line": 293,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> Therefore, we have to probe it twice based on the different AM4 values.\n\nInstance 16 / 17 in this file have the same AM4 values, so I expect you're referring to a different file (which is ok to have another file with different AM4 values).\n\n> Additionally, if we don't explicitly reference bus 16/17 in the probe statement, it's likely that bus 16/17 may not consistently correspond to fan 0/1 when probing the fan board. If my understanding is incorrect, please feel free to let me know, thank you.\n\nI don't think this is correct.  As I mentioned, use `$bus % 2` in the Name and it will always map 16 -> 0 and 17 -> 1.  You should see other EM files that have similar behavior."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configurations/meta/ventura_fanboard_adc_max.json",
                    "line": 293,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jason Hsu",
                        "email": "jason-hsu@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "QuantaJasonHsu"
                    },
                    "message": "Thank you for your suggestion. We have created a separate file with a different AM4 value and replaced the explicit bus 16/17 reference with $bus % 2 in the probe statement, which made the code cleaner."
                }
            ],
